WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.994
Module 9 is about knowledge — giving the model real documents to draw on instead of guessing.

2
00:00:03.994 --> 00:00:07.000
In this one we build our first knowledge base and attach it to a chat.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:11.990
Before you add a single document, pick an embedding model in Settings, Providers, Embeddings.

4
00:00:11.990 --> 00:00:18.000
This choice matters more than it looks — it decides how every document gets indexed, and you'll see why shortly.

5
00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:18.792
Two real options.

6
00:00:18.792 --> 00:00:22.939
Local, with Ollama's nomic-embed-text — free, and your documents never leave the machine.

7
00:00:22.939 --> 00:00:28.857
Cloud, with text-embedding-3-small — cheap per document, but every file you index gets uploaded to the provider to be embedded.

8
00:00:28.857 --> 00:00:31.000
Pick based on how sensitive the documents are.

9
00:00:31.000 --> 00:00:32.063
Now the base itself.

10
00:00:32.063 --> 00:00:37.696
Automation, Knowledge, New knowledge base — give it a name, then add a handful of PDFs and Markdown files.

11
00:00:37.696 --> 00:00:42.000
The app chunks and embeds them in the background using the model you just picked.

12
00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.335
Attach the base to a chat and ask a question that's only answered inside one of your documents.

13
00:00:46.335 --> 00:00:54.000
No explicit search instruction needed — retrieval happens automatically before the model even starts composing a reply, and the answer comes back grounded in your text.

14
00:00:54.000 --> 00:00:57.812
The gotcha: changing the embedding model invalidates every index built with the old one.

15
00:00:57.812 --> 00:01:01.061
And re-indexing is not incremental — it redoes the whole base from scratch.

16
00:01:01.061 --> 00:01:06.000
Pick your embedding model deliberately before you import a large collection, because switching later is expensive.

17
00:01:06.000 --> 00:01:07.006
You've seen retrieval work.

18
00:01:07.006 --> 00:01:12.000
Next we open the hood — chunking, embedding, and nearest-neighbor search — and cover exactly when this approach does and doesn't work.

